## Why This Is Asked
This question probes multi-tenant CNF provenance at scale, including per-tenant keys, drift detection, rollback, and observability.

## Key Concepts
- Tenant-scoped key management
- Per-tenant SBOMs and provenance enforcement
- ImagePolicyWebhook + OPA constraints
- Drift detection and tenant-aware rollback
- Observability: metrics, traces, audit logs

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: verify image provenance per tenant
async function verifyProvenance(imageRef, tenant) {
  const sigOk = await cosign.verify(imageRef, { key: `kms://${tenant}-key` });
  const sbom = await fetchSBOM(imageRef);
  if (sbom.tenant !== tenant) throw new Error('Tenant SBOM mismatch');
  return sigOk;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you rotate tenant keys without downtime?
- How would you test drift detection across regions?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design traffic shaping at the CNF data plane, considering multi-tenant isolation and simple orchestration.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant traffic prioritization
- Sidecar data-plane logic with header-based decisions
- Lightweight state via Redis and a simple token bucket
- Minimal manifests to validate end-to-end

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo in-sidecar router
function route(req){
  const tenant = req.headers['X-TENANT-ID'];
  const pri = req.headers['X-TENANT-PRIORITY'] || 'normal';
  const pathHash = hash(req.path);
  if(pri === 'high') return forwardFast(req);
  const key = `tenant:${tenant}:path:${pathHash}`;
  if(redis.incr(key) > RATE_LIMIT){
    return reject(429, 'Too many requests');
  }
  redis.pexpire(key, 60000);
  return forwardSlow(req);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this with a canary tenant without affecting others?
- What monitoring would you add to ensure fairness during bursts?
